"source_url":"https://os.copernicus.org/articles/14/1461/2018/","source_locator":"Section 2.1 and Figure 1 caption: northwestward eastern-coast EAC, separate southeastward western-coast WAC, three sub-basin recirculations, wind modulation.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"east-adriatic","as_of":"2026-10-03","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://os.copernicus.org/articles/14/1461/2018/","source_access":"publisher_full_text_relevant_background_and_figure_caption_read","source_citation":"Sciascia et al. (2018), Linking sardine recruitment in coastal areas to ocean currents using surface drifters and HF radar: a case study in the Gulf of Manfredonia, Adriatic Sea. Ocean Science 14, 1461-1482, doi:10.5194/os-14-1461-2018.","source_locator":"Section 2.1 and Figure 1 caption: northwestward eastern-coast EAC, separate southeastward western-coast WAC, three sub-basin recirculations, wind modulation.","naming_decision":{"ledger_label":"East Adriatic Current","source_label":"Eastern Adriatic Current","assignment":"Editorial alignment of the same eastern-coast northwestward circulation convention; no new canonical alias admitted.","dalmatian_name_synonymy":"unresolved_not_merged"},"geographic_conventions":[{"url":"https://os.copernicus.org/articles/14/1461/2018/","locator":"Section 2.1 and Figure 1","scope":"Northwestward eastern Balkan coast; distinct western return current and recirculation gyres. The stated 800 by 200 km dimensions describe the Adriatic basin, not the EAC length or width.","access":"publisher_relevant_sections_read","source_license":"Publisher states Creative Commons Attribution 4.0; no source figure or drifter data copied."},{"url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/marine-science/articles/10.3389/fmars.2020.00679/full","locator":"Study area, mean circulation paragraph citing Poulain (2001)","scope":"Background convention extends EAC northward from Otranto toward the Istrian Peninsula. This is regional circulation context, not a diagnosed continuous axis or formal current termination.","access":"publisher_relevant_study_area_text_read","source_license":"Not independently reviewed in this audit; attributed textual facts only."}],"original_measurement_source":{"url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2005JC003271","citation":"Orlic et al. (2006), Wintertime buoyancy forcing, changing seawater properties, and two different circulation systems produced in the Adriatic.","access":"Publisher full text inaccessible in this turn; author-hosted search excerpt found but full page access returned HTTP 429. No new values extracted.","existing_ledger_scope":"2002-2003 local eastern-coast measurements; no full path length admitted."},"layer":"General surface circulation context in the two accessed papers. Intermediate-water inflow is not assigned the same route or a uniform full-depth layer.","observed_period":null,"whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"route_construction_status":"editorial_candidate_constructed_not_canonical","proposed_gate_regions":["Eastern Otranto inflow neighborhood","Offshore western/southern Istrian neighborhood"],"exclusions":["Western Adriatic return flow","Northern, central and southern recirculation loops","Individually unverified island-channel branches","Dalmatian-name extent transfer"],"remaining_gates":["Choose explicit offshore gate coordinates and alternative coastal paths with the same convention.","Screen all declared paths against the coarse land geometry, then review islands and narrow passages using finer bathymetry.","Retain the regional convention and unresolved Dalmatian naming in every length record; do not borrow 800 km basin length.","Retrieve original measurement and drifter papers before claiming a diagnosed axis, seasonal footprint, or numeric current width."],"candidate_file":"research/east-adriatic-reference-p