t_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["pacific-necc-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"Repeated Pacific velocity sections identify a near-surface eastward core around 7°N at 140°W. The section locates the current but gives no comparable along-current length.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","source_decision":{"current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-countercurrent","name":"Pacific North Equatorial Countercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"Repeated Pacific velocity sections identify a near-surface eastward core around 7°N at 140°W. The section locates the current but gives no comparable along-current length.","name_source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","candidate_ids":["pacific-necc-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"eastward upper-ocean countercurrent","route_strategy_id":"expand_section_evidence","route_strategy_label":"Extend evidence beyond sections","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"Repeated Pacific velocity sections identify a near-surface eastward core around 7°N at 140°W. The section locates the current but gives no comparable along-current length.","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","strategy_next_action":"Find sourced upstream/downstream gates and continuity between sections; preserve widths and section transports as different measurements.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[{"note":{"current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-countercurrent","label":"Mean section connection versus seasonal surface expression","audit_file":"research/pacific-necc-reach-seasonal-section-scope-audit.json","summary":"140 W to 95 W is an editorial studied reach, not a whole-current axis. Near 110 W the source surface expression is absent in December-February and peaks in August; this does not establish absence at all depths. Source ensemble dates do not date this route; width and annual ranges remain unresolved.","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","source_locator":"Section 3.1: 140 W near-surface core around 7 N, 125 W around 8 N, southward shift at 110 W and northward rebuilding at 95 W; section 3.2.3 separates seasonal surface expression near 110 W.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-countercurrent","as_of":"2026-10-04","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","source_citation":"Johnson, Sloyan, Kessler and McTaggart (2002), Direct measurements of upper ocean currents and water properties across the tropical Pacific during the 1990s, Progress in Oceanography 52, 31-61, doi:10.1016/S0079-6611(02)00021-6.","source_access":"NOAA-hosted introduction, section 2 and section 3 text read; figures and CTD/ADCP fields not digitized or repackaged. Bibliographic page range 31-61 checked against PMEL publication record; HTML header 31-36 is not adopted.","source_locator":"Section 3.1: 140 W near-surface core around 7 N, 125 W around 8 N, southward shift at 110 W and northward rebuilding at 95 W; section 3.2.3 separates seasonal surface expression near 110 W.","source_ensemble_period_months":{"start":"1985-06","end":"2000-12"},"source_ensemble_period_is_route_date":false,"observed_period":null,"section_longitudes_degrees_east":[-140,-125,-110,-95],"editorial_latitude_choices":{"110W":6,"95W":7},"scope":"Declared east-central Pacific study reach from 140 W to 95 W. Gates truncate a larger current; western Pacific inflow and coastal redistribution east of 95 W are excluded. Intermediate bends are editorial connections among section descriptions, not a reconstructed continuous axis or parcel trajectory.","layer":"Near-surface and upper-ocean eastward countercurrent; maxima can be slightly subsurface. Separate from equatorial EUC and deeper NSCC/Tsuchiya Jet; no uniform depth imposed.","surface_expression_caution":"Near 110 W the fitted source surface expression is absent in December-February and peaks in August; slightly subsurface maxima and NSCC are distinct. No general absence of all eastward upper-ocean flow is claimed.","section_mapping_caution":"ADCP generally begins about 20 m; mapped shear is extrapolated to surface. The source uses annual harmonics/SOI with uneven sampling, not monthly routes extracted here.","source_declared_correlation_scales_are_width":false,"whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"route_construction_status":"editorial_studied_reach_constructed","candidate_file":"research/pacific-necc-reach-reference-path-candidate.json","remaining_gates":["Review editor-selected 110 W/95 W latitudes and interpolating continuity against compatible velocity fields.","Extract depth and month-specific axes before length or width seasonal ranges.","Keep weak or absent surface flow near 110 W separate from subsurface eastward flow; no annual continuous path inferred.","Resolve western inflow and coastal redistribution separately from this truncated studied reach."],"local_section_diagnostic":{"file":"research/pacific-necc-oscar-2013-section-diagnostic.json","visual_url":"almanac/necc-section.html","status":"derived_width_candidate_requires_scientific_review","scope":"140 W archived OSCAR monthly means in 2013; not a whole-current width or reproduction of the source climatology."}},"source_sha256":"e97d4ddb320f9384f527b001c44058f74f3ad0c126d40429f890decfa25553de"}]},{"id":"route-decision:pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","entity_id":"current:pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","label":"Pacific North Equatorial Undercurrent","current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","strategy_label":"Construct layer-specific paths","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":3,"route_ids":["pacific-neuc-middle-reach-reference-path-candidate","pacific-neuc-northern-reach-reference-path-candidate","pacific-neuc-southern-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"The 130°E mooring array measures eastward subsurface jets between 8.5°N and 17.5°N, with 5.1 Sv mean positive transport to 900 m under the study definition. This cross-section is not a path length.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/marine-science/articles/10.3389/fmars.2022.979442/full","source_decision":{"current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","name":"Pacific North Equatorial Undercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"section_only","existing_scope":"The 130°E mooring array measures eastward subsurface jets between 8.5°N and 17.5°N, with 5.1 Sv mean positive transport to 900 m under the study definition. This cross-section is not a path length.","name_source_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/marine-science/articles/10.3389/fmars.2022.979442/full","candidate_ids":["pacific-neuc-middle-reach-reference-path-candidate","pacific-neuc-northern-reach-reference-path-candidate","pacific-neuc-southern-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"intermittent subsurface jets","route_strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","route_strategy_label":"Construct layer-specific paths","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"The 130°E mooring array measures eastward subsurface jets between 8.5°N and 17.5°N, with 5.1 Sv mean positive transport to 900 m under the study definition. This cross-section is not a path length.","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","strategy_next_action":"Specify source layer and time support, obtain intermediate pathway geometry, and separate branches or dilution thresholds before measuring.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[{"note":{"current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","label":"NEUC mooring jets versus northern Tsuchiya support","audit_file":"research/pacific-neuc-nscc-distinction-scope-audit.json","summary":"The 130 E NEUC mooring study concerns multiple subsurface jets farther north than the equatorial northern Tsuchiya cores. No alias equivalence or connecting trajectory is established; the Tsuchiya route length is not transferred. Transport integration bounds do not establish width.","source_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/marine-science/articles/10.3389/fmars.2022.979442/full","source_locator":"Mean structure Results: 130 E multiple eastward jets at 8.5 N/12.5 N/17.5 N; positive velocities integrated from 26.8 sigma-theta to 900 m across 8.5-17.5 N.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-north-equatorial-undercurrent","as_of":"2026-10-03","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/marine-science/articles/10.3389/fmars.2022.979442/full","source_locator":"Mean structure Results: 130 E multiple eastward jets at 8.5 N/12.5 N/17.5 N; positive velocities integrated from 26.8 sigma-theta to 900 m across 8.5-17.5 N.","source_access":"Abstract and relevant mean-structure and limitations paragraphs read. Mooring figures and fields not digitized.","distinction":"NEUC mooring-section support at 130 E is not the northern Tsuchiya Jet mean-core support at 155 W and 110 W. Shared eastward subsurface descriptions do not establish alias equivalence or a connecting trajectory.","comparison_current_id":"pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","comparison_source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","observed_period":null,"transport_integration_bounds_are_current_width":false,"whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"route_construction_status":"intermediate_branch_geometry_required","remaining_gates":["Retain mooring-array support and branch intermittency; do not transfer northern Tsuchiya Jet length or local depths.","Resolve branch-specific intermediate geometry before a route length.","Do not treat 8.5-17.5 N integration span as width or 900 m integration endpoint as core depth."]},"source_sha256":"d41b02c0c981c39afacbecb50b67ee4b9613d691a226c0f980063a830682b564"}]},{"id":"route-decision:pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","entity_id":"current:pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","label":"Pacific North Subsurface Countercurrent","current_id":"pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","strategy_label":"Construct layer-specific paths","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["pacific-nscc-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"A Pacific section at 140°W locates this eastward jet near 4°N and 200 m; the section does not determine its complete alongflow route or length.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","source_decision":{"current_id":"pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","name":"Pacific North Subsurface Countercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"A Pacific section at 140°W locates this eastward jet near 4°N and 200 m; the section does not determine its complete alongflow route or length.","name_source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","candidate_ids":["pacific-nscc-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"eastward subsurface jet north of the Equatorial Undercurrent","route_strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","route_strategy_label":"Construct layer-specific paths","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"A Pacific section at 140°W locates this eastward jet near 4°N and 200 m; the section does not determine its complete alongflow route or length.","next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","strategy_next_action":"Specify source layer and time support, obtain intermediate pathway geometry, and separate branches or dilution thresholds before measuring.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[{"note":{"current_id":"pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","label":"Northern Tsuchiya mean cores and integration limits","audit_file":"research/pacific-nscc-reach-layer-section-scope-audit.json","summary":"155 W to 110 W is an editorial northern Tsuchiya studied reach. Local mean cores shoal from 220 m to 130 m; no uniform axis depth inferred. Transport integration latitude limits are not width. Historical compilation dates do not date the route, and the 130 E NEUC identity is kept separate. A separate Rowe et al. general 2-degree meridional width summary is now retained as approximately 220 km; the two jet records share one source claim, not independent observations or annual extrema.","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","source_locator":"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: north SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 N/220 m and 110 W 4.5 N/130 m. Transport integration latitude limits and 700-dbar reference are separately defined; they are not route width or a uniform axis layer.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-north-subsurface-countercurrent","as_of":"2026-10-04","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","source_citation":"Johnson and Moore (1997), The Pacific Subsurface Countercurrents and an Inertial Model, Journal of Physical Oceanography 27, 2448-2459.","source_access":"Relevant abstract/introduction, data methods, section 3 and text Table 1 read from NOAA-hosted full HTML. No figure digitization, historical CTD extraction or full annual-field reconstruction. Added inspection of Rowe et al. section 2 sampling/boundary methods and section 3b general width statement in NOAA PDF, confirmed in publisher full text; no figures or paired edges digitized.","source_locator":"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: north SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 N/220 m and 110 W 4.5 N/130 m. Transport integration latitude limits and 700-dbar reference are separately defined; they are not route width or a uniform axis layer.","source_ensemble_years":[1967,1996],"source_ensemble_period_is_route_date":false,"observed_period":null,"source_core_support":[{"longitude":-155,"latitude":3.5,"local_peak_depth_m":220},{"longitude":-110,"latitude":4.5,"local_peak_depth_m":130}],"source_core_depths_are_uniform_route_layer":false,"source_transport_integration_bounds":[{"longitude":-155,"latitude_range":[2,5]},{"longitude":-110,"latitude_range":[3,6]}],"transport_integration_bounds_are_current_width":false,"source_geostrophic_reference_dbar":700,"source_reference_is_axis_depth":false,"scope":"Declared northern Tsuchiya Jet studied reach from the 155 W mean-section core to the 110 W mean-section core. Western formation and basin continuation east of 110 W are excluded. The section gates truncate a larger current; interpolating bends are editor-selected, not a measured continuous centerline or particle path.","layer":"Subsurface northern Tsuchiya Jet, distinct from upper-ocean NECC and equatorial EUC. Source Table 1 gives local mean peak depths of 220 m at 155 W and 130 m at 110 W; these are local supports, not a uniform depth or full vertical boundaries.","coordinate_selection":"OSW adopts rounded Table 1 core neighborhoods at 155 W 3.5 N and 110 W 4.5 N as truncation gates. Intermediate 140 W 3.8 N and 125 W 4.2 N are editor-selected connections, not digitized axis points. The path neither assigns a whole-current origin/termination nor merges northern/southern jets or NEUC.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"route_construction_status":"editorial_studied_reach_constructed","candidate_file":"research/pacific-nscc-reach-reference-path-candidate.json","remaining_gates":["Review interpolation against compatible northern Tsuchiya Jet velocity fields; selected section gates are not origins or terminations.","Resolve western formation and eastward turning/merging separately from this studied reach.","Extract month/depth-resolved axes before annual length or width ranges.","Keep NEUC at 130 E and the southern Tsuchiya branches as distinct identities; no transported-volume-to-length or integration-band-to-width conversion."],"reported_ensemble_angular_width":{"source_claim_id":"rowe-2000-scc-general-two-degree-width","source_url":"https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/30/6/1520-0485_2000_030_1172_pescvt_2.0.co_2.xml","source_pdf_url":"https://floats.pmel.noaa.gov/sites/default/files/atoms/files/gcj_1y.pdf","source_locator":"Section 3b, printed 1179 / PDF index 7; boundary methodology section 2, printed 1175.","latitude_span_degrees":2,"scope":"General meridional SCC width in discussion of stream-coordinate averages; not a separate regional value for each jet.","branch_assignment":"NSCC","exact_section_geometry":null,"fixed_depth_bounds_m":null,"observed_period":null,"shared_source_claim":true,"independent_jet_measurements":false,"core_position_variation_is_width_range":false,"pv_front_width_is_current_width":false,"annual_range_supported":false,"boundary_method":"Neutral-layer zonal-velocity sign transition where available; subjective local minimum when adjacent eastward currents merge. Paired edges underlying the general 2-degree statement are not extracted.","sampling_note":"Stream-coordinate averaging; spatial bins have uneven time coverage and depth-dependent sampling."}},"source_sha256":"37bc44a75e6fe3a566d4fb0ba13887a509e9223c73a19b2eb34a651b213b71c7"}]},{"id":"route-decision:pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","entity_id":"current:pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","label":"Pacific South Equatorial Countercurrent","current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","strategy_id":"seasonal_routes","strategy_label":"Construct seasonal routes","construction_status":"reference_path_not_constructed","candidate_count":0,"route_ids":[],"next_action":"Recover compatible numerical total-flow axes and paired boundaries; keep WOA01 surface/0-200 m geostrophy relative to 1500 dbar separate from modeled Sverdrup flow and band-passed anomaly sections at 170 E. Do not use anomaly zero contours as width edges, averaging windows as endpoints, or merge them with the newer Argo envelopes. See research/pacific-secc-woa01-anomaly-scope-audit.json; full 2026 methods and seasonal calendars remain needed.","scope":"Argo-based analysis reports a boreal-spring band near 5–13°S from 150°E to 170°W, with seasonal changes in zonal reach and depth. That observed seasonal envelope is not a traced alongstream length and is unranked.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JC023053","source_decision":{"current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","name":"Pacific South Equatorial Countercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"Argo-based analysis reports a boreal-spring band near 5–13°S from 150°E to 170°W, with seasonal changes in zonal reach and depth. That observed seasonal envelope is not a traced alongstream length and is unranked.","name_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JC023053","candidate_ids":[],"reference_path_decision":"reference_path_not_constructed","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"seasonally varying eastward countercurrent","route_strategy_id":"seasonal_routes","route_strategy_label":"Construct seasonal routes","planning_status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","planning_basis":"Argo-based analysis reports a boreal-spring band near 5–13°S from 150°E to 170°W, with seasonal changes in zonal reach and depth. That observed seasonal envelope is not a traced alongstream length and is unranked.","next_action":"Recover compatible numerical total-flow axes and paired boundaries; keep WOA01 surface/0-200 m geostrophy relative to 1500 dbar separate from modeled Sverdrup flow and band-passed anomaly sections at 170 E. Do not use anomaly zero contours as width edges, averaging windows as endpoints, or merge them with the newer Argo envelopes. See research/pacific-secc-woa01-anomaly-scope-audit.json; full 2026 methods and seasonal calendars remain needed.","strategy_next_action":"Choose sourced season, direction and layer; construct each supported seasonal path separately and preserve missing seasons.","known_component_currents":[],"component_inventory_status":"not_assessed_by_this_planning_crosswalk"},"source_catalog_file":"research/ocean-current-reference-path-candidates.json","source_catalog_sha256":"b06caed27655c39f8127ddda441cba0a856800f599ec52edcd5b30d2ad37b633","scope_reviews":[{"note":{"current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","label":"Argo seasonal envelope and dateline scope","audit_file":"research/pacific-secc-argo-seasonal-extent-scope-audit.json","summary":"Source preview reports a spring envelope from 150 E to 170 W and an autumn extension to 140 W. These dateline-crossing spans are not traced current lengths. Spring latitude limits are not paired current edges; full methods, averaging years and calendar months remain unresolved.","source_url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2025JC023053?af=R","source_locator":"Publisher abstract, Plain Language Summary and Data Availability Statement.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","status":"editorial_source_scope_not_scientific_approval","source_retrieved_date":"2026-10-04","source_url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2025JC023053?af=R","source_citation":"Wu, Yang and Liang (2026), Seasonal and Interannual Variability of the Pacific South Equatorial Countercurrent Based on Argo Profiles, doi:10.1029/2025JC023053.","source_locator":"Publisher abstract, Plain Language Summary and Data Availability Statement.","source_access":"Publisher preview inspected. Full methods, figures and supplementary file not acquired; study averaging years and calendar-month definitions unresolved.","scope":"Reported seasonal geographic envelopes from Argo-derived absolute geostrophic currents; not a measured current axis or velocity-boundary width.","observed_period":null,"phases":[{"label":"Boreal spring","calendar_months":null,"reported_longitude_limits_degrees_east":[150,-170],"eastward_longitude_span_degrees":40,"crosses_dateline":true,"reported_latitude_limits_degrees":[-13,-5],"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"source_locator":"Abstract"},{"label":"Autumn (source label)","calendar_months":null,"reported_longitude_limits_degrees_east":[150,-140],"eastward_longitude_span_degrees":70,"crosses_dateline":true,"reported_latitude_limits_degrees":null,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"source_locator":"Abstract"}],"geographic_rule":"Longitude limits are source-envelope gates, not flow origin/termination. Spring latitude limits are not paired current edges. No rectangle or midpoint line is an observed footprint or axis. Dateline differences use eastward modulo 360, never a 320/290 degree westward span.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"seasonal_route_playback_supported":false,"remaining_gates":["Inspect full methods and source season definitions; recover averaging years and exact month membership.","Recover source-supported longitudinal axes and compatible layer/velocity boundaries before length or width calculation.","Separate ENSO composites from seasonal climatology and justify named-flow endpoints.","Independent scientific and canonical admission review."]},"source_sha256":"09cd0ecd3e76bdb88266cd8543c87b55465cc9015446b32ae2330cbd85fe2995"},{"note":{"current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","label":"WOA01 mean flow and seasonal anomaly separation","audit_file":"research/pacific-secc-woa01-anomaly-scope-audit.json","summary":"Full 2004 paper distinguishes surface and 0-200 m WOA01 geostrophy relative to 1500 dbar, modeled Sverdrup flow, and band-passed seasonal anomaly sections at 170 E. Anomaly zero contours and averaging windows cannot define current widths or endpoints. Numerical compatible flow fields remain needed.","source_url":"https://www.soest.hawaii.edu/oceanography/bo/CQ04.pdf","source_locator":"Figures 1, 2 and 8; paragraphs 2-3, 20 and 26.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent","status":"editorial_source_scope_not_scientific_approval","source_retrieved_date":"2026-10-04","source_url":"https://www.soest.hawaii.edu/oceanography/bo/CQ04.pdf","source_citation":"Chen and Qiu (2004), Seasonal variability of the South Equatorial Countercurrent, JGR 109 C08003, doi:10.1029/2003JC002243.","source_locator":"Paragraphs 2-3, 20, 26 and 34; Figures 1, 2, 5 and 8 captions; PDF pages 1-3, 6, 8-9 and 11.","source_access":"University-hosted full PDF acquired; relevant text and rendered Figures 1 and 8 inspected. No numerical field or contour trace extracted. The newer Wu et al. (2026) methods were not acquired through the inspected publisher paths.","source_pdf_file":"research/source-data/pacific-secc-chen-qiu-2004.pdf","source_pdf_sha256":"3c194db5c94961aafc89d4e33011384cdedabaf2d9551941c1a659fd959c3643","source_pdf_bytes":946060,"source_pdf_pages":12,"scope":"Keep mean relative geostrophy, modeled flow and seasonal anomaly sections distinct before route or width extraction.","observed_period":null,"mean_geographic_context":{"reported_latitude_band_degrees":[-11,-8],"role":"regional_context_not_paired_velocity_edges","source_locator":"Paragraph 2"},"field_support":[{"id":"woa01-surface-relative-geostrophy","source_locator":"Figure 1a","quantity":"mean_zonal_geostrophic_velocity","product":"WOA01","layer":"surface","reference_pressure_dbar":1500,"is_total_relative_velocity":true,"is_velocity_anomaly":false,"source_sign_encoding":"Positive eastward velocity shaded; contours in cm/s.","numerical_field_acquired":false,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"paired_velocity_boundary_coordinates_lon_lat":null},{"id":"woa01-upper-200m-relative-geostrophy","source_locator":"Figure 1b","quantity":"mean_zonal_geostrophic_velocity","product":"WOA01","layer":"0-200 m average","reference_pressure_dbar":1500,"is_total_relative_velocity":true,"is_velocity_anomaly":false,"numerical_field_acquired":false,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"paired_velocity_boundary_coordinates_lon_lat":null},{"id":"ers-sverdrup-model","source_locator":"Figure 1c","quantity":"modeled_zonal_sverdrup_flow","product":"10-year ERS wind mean","layer":"Transport assigned to upper 200 m by model assumption","is_total_relative_velocity":false,"is_velocity_anomaly":false,"numerical_field_acquired":false,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"paired_velocity_boundary_coordinates_lon_lat":null},{"id":"woa01-local-monthly-mean","source_locator":"Figure 2","quantity":"area_averaged_surface_relative_zonal_geostrophic_velocity","product":"Monthly WOA01 temperature and salinity","longitude_window_degrees_east":[170,180],"latitude_window_degrees":[-13,-8],"window_role":"averaging_domain_not_current_edges_or_endpoints","whole_current_representative":false,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"paired_velocity_boundary_coordinates_lon_lat":null},{"id":"band-passed-monthly-section","source_locator":"Paragraphs 20 and 26; Figure 8","quantity":"seasonal_zonal_geostrophic_velocity_from_band_passed_ssh","longitude_degrees_east":170,"layer":"surface","observation_branch":"altimetric SSH anomaly","model_branch":"1.5-layer forced Rossby-wave SSH","band_pass_period_days":[150,500],"averaging_duration_source":"More than eight years","exact_averaging_interval":null,"is_velocity_anomaly":true,"is_total_relative_velocity":false,"source_sign_encoding":"Negative anomalies shaded; velocity contours in cm/s.","width_extraction_admissible":false,"axis_coordinates_lon_lat":null,"paired_velocity_boundary_coordinates_lon_lat":null}],"historical_comparison":{"source_locator":"Paragraph 3; summaries of Gouriou and Toole (1993) and Reverdin et al. (1994)","underlying_originals_inspected":false,"comparability":"Different longitudes, layers, periods and methods; seasonal signs cannot be pooled into a uniform named-current calendar."},"geographic_rule":"Reference pressure is not current-axis depth. Geographic/averaging windows are not paired edges. Model boundaries and zero velocity-anomaly contours do not establish total-flow boundaries; do not add the WOA01 mean to the differently referenced altimetric anomaly field without reviewed compatibility.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"seasonal_route_playback_supported":false,"remaining_gates":["Acquire compatible numerical mean/monthly velocity fields and resolve their reference state, averaging periods and uncertainty.","Recover longitudinal connected velocity-core support and source-defined gates before tracing a route; do not bridge separated positive components.","Use paired total-flow boundaries for local width extraction; anomaly zero crossings remain inadmissible as current-width edges.","Keep 2004 relative-geostrophic support separate from the 2026 Argo absolute-geostrophic envelopes and ENSO composites.","Independent scientific boundary, identity and canonical admission review."]},"source_sha256":"0a98a08b0191c3450c9e5b4146299c411e724b9ffd6a267cbf548587a5d10051"}]},{"id":"route-decision:pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","entity_id":"current:pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","label":"Pacific South Subsurface Countercurrent","current_id":"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","strategy_label":"Construct layer-specific paths","construction_status":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","candidate_count":1,"route_ids":["pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"next_action":"Review the existing editorial route against scientific gates; broader/system/family scope remains governed by the planning strategy.","scope":"The Pacific 140°W section resolves two south-side branches near 4.5°S at 200 m and 7°S at 250 m. These section positions do not supply a whole-current path length or a single branch-averaged centerline.","status":"editorial_planning_crosswalk_not_scientific_classification_approval","rank_eligible":false,"source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","source_decision":{"current_id":"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","name":"Pacific South Subsurface Countercurrent","existing_length_evidence_status":"no_numeric_length","existing_scope":"The Pacific 140°W section resolves two south-side branches near 4.5°S at 200 m and 7°S at 250 m. These section positions do not supply a whole-current path length or a single branch-averaged centerline.","name_source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml","candidate_ids":["pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate"],"reference_path_decision":"editorial_candidate_present_scientific_review_pending","published_length_rank_eligible":false,"ledger_kind":"eastward subsurface jets south of the Equatorial Undercurrent","route_strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","route_strategy_label":"Construct la