troduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.","observed_period":null},"document":{"schema":"osw.current-source-scope-audit.v1","current_id":"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent","as_of":"2026-10-04","status":"editorial_source_scope_audit_not_canonical","source_url":"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml","source_citation":"Johnson and Moore (1997), The Pacific Subsurface Countercurrents and an Inertial Model, Journal of Physical Oceanography 27, 2448-2459.","source_access":"Johnson and Moore sections 2-3/text Table 1 read; Rowe et al. introduction, data-method passages and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption read from primary NOAA-hosted PDF. Figures not digitized; no full-paper read or field extraction claimed. Added inspection of Rowe et al. section 2 sampling/boundary methods and section 3b general width statement in NOAA PDF, confirmed in publisher full text; no figures or paired edges digitized.","source_locator":"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: south SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 S/250 m and 110 W 5.5 S/160 m. Latitude integration bands are not widths. Rowe et al. (2000), introduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.","source_ensemble_years":[1967,1996],"source_ensemble_period_is_route_date":false,"observed_period":null,"source_core_support":[{"longitude":-155,"latitude":-3.5,"local_peak_depth_m":250},{"longitude":-110,"latitude":-5.5,"local_peak_depth_m":160}],"source_core_depths_are_uniform_route_layer":false,"source_transport_integration_bounds":[{"longitude":-155,"latitude_range":[-8,-3]},{"longitude":-110,"latitude_range":[-6,-3]}],"transport_integration_bounds_are_current_width":false,"source_named_branches":[{"source_label":"primary SSCC / main SSCC","source":"Rowe et al. (2000)","support":"Fig. 10 caption describes a primary core around 4 S at 155 W; not assigned automatically to historical mean-core axis."},{"source_label":"secondary SSCC","source":"Rowe et al. (2000)","support":"Fig. 10 caption describes a secondary core around 6.5 S at 155 W; section 3e retains uncertainty about independent origin versus splitting."}],"source_branch_topology_status":"independent_origin_versus_split_unresolved","historical_mean_core_to_named_branch_assignment":"not_established","source_front_scale":{"variable":"potential_vorticity_front","approximate_span_km":40,"bound_kind":"likely_upper_bound_due_to_density_resolution","is_whole_current_width":false,"is_annual_width_range":false,"source_locator":"Rowe et al. (2000) pp. 1174-1175, methods: stream-coordinate averaging, density resolution and front-scale limitation."},"scope":"Declared historical southern mean-core studied reach from 155 W 3.5 S to 110 W 5.5 S. These gates truncate the larger southern subsurface flow. Western formation, secondary-jet geometry and eastern continuation are excluded. This is one study-defined connection, not an aggregate centerline or sum of primary and secondary branches.","layer":"Southern subsurface/Tsuchiya flow. Johnson and Moore (1997) Table 1 gives local mean peak depths 250 m at 155 W and 160 m at 110 W; no uniform depth or full vertical bounds imposed. Later observations resolve primary and deeper secondary jets; their correspondence to this historical mean-core route remains a review gate.","coordinate_selection":"OSW uses rounded Table 1 mean-core neighborhoods at 155 W 3.5 S and 110 W 5.5 S as truncation gates. Intermediate 140 W 4.2 S and 125 W 4.8 S are editor-selected. No source figure, current axis or dated particle path digitized. Correspondence with later primary/secondary branch labels is not assumed.","whole_current_length_km":null,"whole_current_width_km":null,"annual_length_range_km":null,"annual_width_range_km":null,"route_construction_status":"editorial_historical_mean_core_studied_reach_constructed","candidate_file":"research/pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate.json","remaining_gates":["Review correspondence of historical mean-core sections to later primary/secondary labels; no branch aggregation inferred.","Review interpolation with compatible southern-jet velocity fields and independently resolve secondary-jet geometry.","Obtain month/depth axes before seasonal dimensions; source compilation years are not route observation dates.","Resolve western formation and eastern continuation; transport bands and PV-front spans must remain separate from current width."],"reported_ensemble_angular_width":{"source_claim_id":"rowe-2000-scc-general-two-degree-width","source_url":"https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/30/6/1520-0485_2000_030_1172_pescvt_2.0.co_2.xml","source_pdf_url":"https://floats.pmel.noaa.gov/sites/default/files/atoms/files/gcj_1y.pdf","source_locator":"Section 3b, printed 1179 / PDF index 7; boundary methodology section 2, printed 1175.","latitude_span_degrees":2,"scope":"General meridional SCC width in discussion of stream-coordinate averages; not a separate regional value for each jet.","branch_assignment":"main SSCC; secondary branch width unextracted","exact_section_geometry":null,"fixed_depth_bounds_m":null,"observed_period":null,"shared_source_claim":true,"independent_jet_measurements":false,"core_position_variation_is_width_range":false,"pv_front_width_is_current_width":false,"annual_range_supported":false,"boundary_method":"Neutral-layer zonal-velocity sign transition where available; subjective local minimum when adjacent eastward currents merge. Paired edges underlying the general 2-degree statement are not extracted.","sampling_note":"Stream-coordinate averaging; spatial bins have uneven time coverage and depth-dependent sampling."}},"source_sha256":"8b7bb77b97b52fb8bca50aab8ff886f8302d3ff792175edf8494a1bc65d41ec2"}]},{"id":"route-decision:persian-gulf-saline-overflow","entity_id":"current:persian-gulf-saline-overflow","label":"Persian Gulf saline overflow","current_id":"persian-gulf-saline-overflow","strategy_id":"layer_specific_route","str